# Vectors of tests/data/sst68000 (SingleStepTests/680x0 68000/v1) on which the
# core follows the manual against the corpus. Format and rules: tests/SstKnown.h
# (@SR lines only): the corpus's final SR is replaced by the manual's, which must
# differ from it in X and C alone, and the ENTIRE final state is then compared;
# a listed vector that fails, or that the corpus no longer holds, fails the gate.
#
# ASR past the operand width (2026-11-24, CHANGELOG 2026-11-24 (second)).
# [B] M68000PRM-1992 ASR (PDF p. 126, manual p. 4-22): "X - Set according to the
# last bit shifted out of the operand; unaffected for a shift count of zero.",
# "C - Set according to the last bit shifted out of the operand; cleared for a
# shift count of zero."; the sign bit is shifted into the high-order bit, and a
# register count is taken modulo 64 (PDF p. 125). The manual has no sentence for
# a count beyond the width: past it every bit shifted out is the sign, so X and
# C are the sign. Table 3-18 (PDF p. 90) gives C = D(r-1), undefined there.
# [D] SingleStepTests/680x0 68000/v1 (e0d5ece9) clears X and C for every count
# beyond the width (9-63 .b, 17-63 .w, 33-63 .l). It disagrees with the manual
# only when the operand is negative: these 3 736 register-form vectors (1 642
# .b, 1 063 .w, 1 031 .l). Dirk's core gives the sign; the queue patch that
# cleared it for the corpus was dropped on 2026-11-24 and no 68000 machine gate
# moved. On the 68020+ both 68030 oracles keep the sign (ruling D10, 2026-07-15).
# [F] What a real 68000 does here is not settled by any source of this tree.
# Settled by: a capture of ASR Dn,Dm with Dn >= 9 on a negative byte on a real
# 68000 (grade A); a matching corpus would then drop this list or the manual's
# reading.
# Generated by tools/sst68000_asr_known.py (reads the corpus, not the core).
